{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791051381,"nonce":"9APHmRy/9HWr2PLsfD+s9cS9XSk=","title":"Hot reload is slow on the NUC: 62 s to start with --hot-reload, 30 s to apply a 56-function edit","message":"The same ashui window program as the crash issue (ashui tools/demo/Reloading.hx), on the NUC (Intel Iris Xe, 6.8 GB):\n\n- `ash --mode hybrid --hot-reload Reloading.hl`: 62 s from launch to the first interactive frame, since --hot-reload forces the LLVM tier for everything (\"[tiered] hot-reload active: forcing --jit-tier=llvm\").\n- Swapping in heading.hl (one string literal changed in a template): 30 s from the file's rename to \"[hot-reload] reloaded 56 changed function(s)\".\n\nThe Haxe side of an edit takes about 2 s, so the edit-to-screen time is almost all Ash's. For a development loop the reload should land in a second or two, and startup should not be much slower than without the flag: e.g. start on the normal tiers and compile only the changed functions on reload (Cranelift, or the interpreter until LLVM catches up), rather than LLVM-compiling the whole program up front.\n\nBuilds and steps: /Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/ashui/.ashui/hot-reload-repro on the Mac, ~/.cache/ashui-nuc/reload on the NUC (before.hl, then rename heading.hl over Reloading.hl), as in the crash issue.","files":null}]}